# Floe — Game state

This file fixes the state the game holds. The debugging surface's readings
project exactly this state, so it is what a scenario poses and reads back;
`specs/instrumentation.md` fixes that surface and the snapshot it returns.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the state entirely. How it is organized, how the two
bands' rosters are held, and which values are stored rather than derived are
yours to decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following, because the rest of this
specification is written against them, and it returns to its title-screen values
when the debugging surface's `reset` is called.

| It carries | |
| --- | --- |
| `screen` | The screen being shown, one of the six in `specs/ui.md`. |
| `phase`, `phaseTimer` | The crossing's phase and the seconds left in the hold it is on, as `specs/progression.md` fixes. |
| `menuIndex` | The highlighted item of the current screen's menu, from `0`. |
| `level`, `reachedLevel` | The level being played, and the highest the run has reached. |
| `lives`, `score` | The lives left counting the critter crossing, and the run's score. |
| `timer` | The seconds left on the crossing timer. |
| The bays | Which of the five are filled, left to right, and which bay the bonus catch is in. |
| The critter | Whether it is on the strait, its center in stage units, its facing, its hop cooldown, and its `bestRow`. |
| The bears | For each: an id, the tile it last settled on, the tile it is travelling into, its center, its facing, the tile it is hunting, and its three faculties. |
| The lanes | For each of the sixteen: its row, its direction, and its speed in tiles per second. |
| The vehicles and the floes | For each: an id, its row, its kind, its left edge, and its length in tiles. |
| The four world gates | `bearEmergence`, `catchTest`, `fishCadence`, and `timerRunning`, each `true` at a fresh start. |
| `simTime` | Accumulated simulation time in seconds. Every tick adds `TICK_DT`, whatever the screen. |
| `muted` | The runtime's mute bit, refreshed from the runtime in every update. |
| The generator | The state of the seeded random generator the lanes' phases and the bonus catch are drawn from. |

Every entity id is a number, unique among the entities live at any moment. An
entity keeps its id for as long as it is on the strait, its lanes wrapping at an
edge included.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` with every field below,
under these names and types. The state is a value the engine holds and every
frame replaces, so every field and every array is declared `readonly`: a frame
builds the next state from the current one rather than writing into it, and every
reader is handed the state as `DeepReadonly<FloeState>` in any case.

Anything else the game must keep from one tick to the next is a field you add to
`FloeState`, rather than a module-level variable or a closure.

```ts
export type Screen =
  "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "victory" | "gameover";

export type Phase = "crossing" | "dying" | "clearing";

export type Facing = "up" | "down" | "left" | "right";

export type LaneDir = 1 | -1;

export type VehicleKind = "plow" | "dogsled" | "car";

export type FloeKind = "pan" | "raft3" | "raft4";

export interface CritterState {
  readonly present: boolean;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly facing: Facing;
  readonly hopCooldown: number;
  readonly bestRow: number;
}

export interface BearState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly col: number;
  readonly row: number;
  readonly stepCol: number;
  readonly stepRow: number;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly facing: Facing;
  readonly target: { readonly col: number; readonly row: number };
  readonly sense: boolean;
  readonly routing: boolean;
  readonly travel: boolean;
}

export interface LaneState {
  readonly row: number;
  readonly dir: LaneDir;
  readonly speed: number;
}

export interface VehicleState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly row: number;
  readonly kind: VehicleKind;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly len: number;
}

export interface FloeItemState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly row: number;
  readonly kind: FloeKind;
  readonly x: number;
  readonly len: number;
}

export interface GateState {
  readonly bearEmergence: boolean;
  readonly catchTest: boolean;
  readonly fishCadence: boolean;
  readonly timerRunning: boolean;
}

export interface FloeState {
  readonly screen: Screen;
  readonly phase: Phase;
  readonly phaseTimer: number;
  readonly menuIndex: number;

  readonly level: number;
  readonly reachedLevel: number;
  readonly lives: number;
  readonly score: number;
  readonly timer: number;

  readonly bays: readonly boolean[];
  readonly fishBay: number | null;

  readonly critter: CritterState;
  readonly bears: readonly BearState[];

  readonly iceLanes: readonly LaneState[];
  readonly waterLanes: readonly LaneState[];
  readonly vehicles: readonly VehicleState[];
  readonly floes: readonly FloeItemState[];

  readonly gates: GateState;

  readonly simTime: number;
  readonly muted: boolean;
  readonly rngState: number;
}
```
{{else}}
The game's state lives in the framework objects the engine owns: the game
instance you write, the world that is open, that world's game mode and game
state, and the actors in it. Floe runs in one world for the whole session, so the
game definition registers the single level `LEVELS` names, the game never opens
another, and every screen is a value of `screen`. Nothing lives in a
module-level variable or a closure outside those objects.

`src/game.ts` declares and exports `FloeState`, the world's game state, as a
subclass of the engine's `GameState`. It is live and written in place, and it
carries the run's own figures:

```ts
export type Screen =
  "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "victory" | "gameover";

export type Phase = "crossing" | "dying" | "clearing";

export type LaneDir = 1 | -1;

export interface LaneState {
  row: number;
  dir: LaneDir;
  speed: number;
}

export class FloeState extends GameState {
  screen: Screen = "title";
  phase: Phase = "crossing";
  phaseTimer = 0;
  menuIndex = 0;

  level = 1;
  reachedLevel = 1;
  lives = START_LIVES;
  score = 0;
  timer = crossingTimer(1);

  bays: boolean[] = [false, false, false, false, false];
  fishBay: number | null = null;

  iceLanes: LaneState[] = [];
  waterLanes: LaneState[] = [];

  bearEmergence = true;
  catchTest = true;
  fishCadence = true;
  timerRunning = true;

  simTime = 0;
  rngState = DEFAULT_SEED;
}
```

Anything else the run must keep between ticks is a field you add to it.

The bodies on the strait are actors in the world, each carrying its tag from
`TAGS`, so the critter, the bears, the vehicles, the floes, and the bonus catch
are found with `world.byTag` under the names this specification uses. An actor's
transform is its position, in the conventions `specs/overview.md` fixes: the
critter's, a bear's, and the bonus catch's is a center, and a vehicle's or a
floe's is its left edge.

Every entity id is a number, unique among the entities live at any moment. An
entity keeps its id for as long as it is on the strait, its lanes wrapping at an
edge included.

The mute bit is the engine's own, read live from the world's audio rather than
copied onto the state.
{{/if}}

## The screens

`screen` is the top-level state machine. `specs/ui.md` fixes what each screen
shows and how the game moves between them.

| `screen` | The game is |
| --- | --- |
| `title` | On the title screen and its menu. |
| `howto` | On the how-to-play screen. |
| `playing` | In live play. |
| `paused` | Paused over a frozen strait. |
| `victory` | On the victory screen, the run won. |
| `gameover` | On the game-over screen, the run lost. |

## The derived facts

Four facts the game reports are derived rather than stored, so they follow
whatever the state holds:

| Fact | Derived from |
| --- | --- |
| The critter's tile | Its center, through `colAt` and `rowAt`. A bear's two tiles are held rather than derived. |
| The critter's footing | Its row and the floes on it, as `specs/strait.md` fixes. |
| A bear's `swimming` flag | The tile it is travelling into, as `specs/hunter.md` fixes. |
| `timerMax` | `crossingTimer(level)`, as `specs/progression.md` fixes. |

## The contract

- Every field above is present from the moment the game has initialized, and none
  of them is optional.
- Anything the build keeps beyond the facts above is refreshed by every operation
  that could invalidate it, so posing one field leaves the game consistent.
- A `reset` restores the facts above to their title-screen values and reseeds the
  game's random generator, leaving the game indistinguishable from one freshly
  started, so anything else the build keeps across ticks follows from what a
  reset restores.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<FloeState>`, to `update`,
  to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation of the
  debugging surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new `FloeState`
  built from the one they received; `render`, the sources, and the surface's
  readings return no state.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}
- The state is written only through the paths play runs on: the player
  controller resolves the tick's input into it, the game mode's tick advances it,
  and the debugging surface's poses arrange it at the call. Rendering reads it
  and writes nothing.
{{/if}}
